<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muri Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1027</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Muri Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muri Abbey is a former Benedictine monastery at Muri in the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, founded in 1027 by Ita of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Count Radbot as the family monastery of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Its monks helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a> Abbey in 1120. The Aargau government dissolved it in 1841 and the community moved to Gries near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>, but the Habsburg link endures: the hearts of the last Austrian emperor Charles I and Empress Zita rest in its church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muri-abbey.jpg" alt="Muri Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_muri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The monastery was dedicated to Saint Martin of Tours and lay in the Diocese of Basel, originally in that of Constance. Its first monks came from nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsiedeln-abbey">Einsiedeln Abbey</a> under Prior Reginbold, on land given by a daughter of Frederick, Duke of Lower Lorraine, and by Werner, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Strasburg. On Reginbold's death in 1055 Burchard was chosen as the first abbot, and the abbey church was consecrated under him in 1064. A further colony of monks came from the Abbey of St. Blaise in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>; one of them, the Blessed Luitfrid, governed both communities until his death on 31 December 1096.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Abbot Henry de Schoenwerd a whole family took vows, the father and sons in the abbey and the mother and daughters in the neighbouring convent of nuns, later moved to Hermetschwil. In 1114 Emperor Henry V took Muri under his protection, and it held lands and rights in Unterwalden, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans">Stans</a> and Buochs and in the mountains around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelberg">Engelberg</a>. Fires in 1300 and 1363 and the war between the Swiss Confederacy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> set it back. Abbot George Russinger, who had attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, joined it to the reforming Congregation of Bursfelde, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> granted its abbots the use of pontificalia.</p><p>In the 1530s troops from Bern, newly Protestant, attacked the abbey, which survived under Abbot Laurentius von Heidegg, a friend of the Zürich reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a>. After the rule of Abbot Jakob Meyer proved an economic disaster, he was forced out in 1596 and replaced by John Jodoc Singisen, who restored discipline and helped found the Swiss Congregation of 1602. His successor Dominic Tschudi wrote the Origo et genealogia comitum de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>. Emperor Leopold I raised Abbot Placid Zurlauben and his successor to princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and paid for rebuilding the monastery and church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The abbey church holds the oldest burial place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and the bodies of Rudolf and Felix, sons of the last emperor, lie with their parents' hearts in the family crypt of the Loreto Chapel. The former abbey is now a museum and cultural centre, showing the treasures of the monastery and the work of the Swiss landscape painter Caspar Wolf, and it is a heritage site of national significance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muri Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Radbot's descendants took the name Habsburg and built up lands in the Aargau, Alsace and around Zurich, founding the family monastery of <b>Muri</b> in 1027.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Conrad of Sellenbüren founded the Benedictine abbey of Engelberg in 1120 in a remote valley below Mount Titlis, with monks from <b>Muri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-engelberg">Imperial Abbey of Engelberg</a></li></ul></section>
